  {
    "submission": "submission-recovery-trigger.json",
    "summary": "Turn 49 drove the executor's timeout path for real and submitted it as the closing check for the liveness question.\n\nWhat was observed locally. A harness blocks row 1 at instance 0 with a record the replica holds but which has no commands and no commit (status 0, crtInstance 0, ExecedUpTo -1) and starts the real executeCommands goroutine, reading only the recovery channel. The blocked instance arrives there: recovery_enqueued with enqueued true for instance 1.0 after a wall-clock wait of about 12.6 seconds, which is the loop's built-in one-millisecond-per-iteration grace counter reaching its limit.\n\nNew obligation and check (claim C8-timeout-enqueues-blocked-instance, bounded liveness, priority 2). The duty is that a replica whose executor finds an instance it cannot apply must enqueue that instance for recovery once the grace period elapses, so the row does not stay blocked without any client stimulus. The harness prints a prerequisite event with the blocked record's status, command presence, crtInstance and ExecedUpTo, then asserts enqueued == true on the recovery event, with a new binding (BB9-timeout-trigger) on the executor loop and instance identity correlation. The claim's scope is one blocked row with the executor running; what the recovery then does and the client-visible effect are excluded, and the wall-clock wait is recorded as a diagnostic rather than a specification.\n\nWhy it matters for the run. This closes the last unexercised item of the liveness question: it establishes the alternative progress path that the dead rejection-branch finding (C7) relies on, because a rejected accept round waits for exactly this enqueue, and it measured the delay a stalled row actually incurs (~12.6s) instead of citing the constant. The question is framed on the per-instance record fact with B9-recovery-trigger as the selected behavior and A2 as the activity, matching the map's own relations.\n\nState. Audit spec version 11; the seven settled claims and their scopes are untouched, the two paused candidates keep their resume conditions, and the only visible bookkeeping item remains the scope issue on the superseded C1 artifact. No requirement, premise or earlier observation was weakened."
